ZTEXT0010000142d05e40b1c0000000
Private Linux host API backend candidate

linux-tables.zero.part contains original x86-64 encoding data expressed in Zero.
It introduces no assembler, C library, external runtime or foreign source code.
The common parser/metadata integration belongs to compiler-host.zero; this part
overrides template IDs 86..101 only for Linux targets 0/1. It also extends the
Linux hosted prologue to preserve the original argc/argv frame in R14, which
existing cooperative contexts already save and restore. The established frame
offsets remain unchanged. Windows and guest templates are separate backends.

All paths are NUL-terminated byte strings. UTF-8 names are passed to Linux
unchanged. Kernel pathname copying bounds every path to 4096 bytes including
the terminator; a longer or inaccessible path returns -1. As with other Zero
pointer primitives, argument[] returns a process-owned pointer, not a copy.
fileopen uses O_CLOEXEC and O_NOFOLLOW: a final symlink is rejected, while
symlinks in parent path components retain normal Linux resolution semantics.
It also uses O_NONBLOCK and verifies S_IFREG through fstat: directories,
devices, sockets and FIFOs are rejected, without blocking on a FIFO open.
Mode 2 truncates only after this validation succeeds. A failed validation
closes the newly opened descriptor. All later I/O remains regular-file I/O.
This is not a directory sandbox or a file-permission elevation mechanism.

host[] = 1. argcount[] excludes the executable name. argument[i] is one-based;
zero or out-of-range indices return zero. Arguments remain available after
cooperative fork/switch because R14 preserves the original process vector.

fileopen[path | mode] returns a nonnegative descriptor or unsigned u64 -1.
0: existing read-only; 1: exclusive create/write; 2: create/truncate/write;
3: existing read/write; 4: exclusive create/write executable. Create modes
1/2 use 0600 and mode 4 uses 0700, each reduced by the caller's umask. Mode 2
does not alter the permissions of an existing file.
No unknown mode is accepted, and no failure falls back to destructive opening.

fileread/ filewrite[handle | buffer | count] retry EINTR and otherwise return
the actual count or -1. Short transfers are exposed; callers must loop to
complete a whole-file operation. A zero read denotes EOF. The file offset is
advanced by the successful count. Descriptor values above INT_MAX are rejected
instead of truncated to another descriptor. Buffer validation is performed by
the kernel. Existing pread/pwrite positioned APIs remain separate and unchanged.

filesize[handle] uses fstat and leaves the offset unchanged. fileclose[handle]
returns 0 or -1; it does not retry EINTR because Linux can release the descriptor
before reporting a later close error. The caller must not reuse a closed handle.
filesync[handle] calls fsync(74), retrying EINTR and normalizing other errors
to -1. It rejects descriptors greater than INT_MAX. The open file offset and
ownership remain unchanged. This requests file flushing only; it does not
flush parent directory entries or guarantee atomic crash-safe publication.
hostwait[milliseconds] uses nanosleep(35) with a private timespec on the native
stack. Values0..86400000 are accepted; other u64 values return -1 immediately.
An EINTR retry uses the remaining timespec. Other failures return -1 and
restore the original stack. This suspends the current native thread, not a
cooperative Zero task. Scheduler latency may extend the requested interval.
filedelete[path] unlinks the named file/link. makedir[path] creates one directory
with mode 0777 reduced by umask. dirempty[path] removes an empty directory.
These namespace operations return 0 or -1.

filemove[source | destination] uses renameat2 with RENAME_NOREPLACE. It never
overwrites an existing destination. Same-filesystem moves are supported;
cross-filesystem moves fail without an implicit copy. Linux must support
renameat2 (introduced in Linux 3.15), and the filesystem must support NOREPLACE.
An unsupported operation returns -1 without an overwrite fallback.

No fd table or hidden global allocator is added. Every native template restores
its stack and preserves the Zero frame, arena and cooperative-context registers.

hostalloc[bytes] uses a private anonymous RW mapping and returns its pointer,
or 0 on failure. hostprotect[pointer | bytes | execute] switches whole pages
to RX when execute=1, or back to RW when execute=0. Other values are rejected;
RWX is never requested. hostfree[pointer | bytes] unmaps. Protection/free return
0/-1; pointers must be page-aligned as required by Linux. The caller owns the
mapping lifetime and must not execute unmapped or writable image memory.

program[] reads /proc/self/exe into a fresh 4096-byte reservation in the current
Zero arena. It returns a NUL-terminated path or 0 for missing procfs, inaccessible
data, truncation or insufficient arena capacity. Successful calls consume one
reservation; failed calls do not advance the arena. A deleted executable can
have Linux's " (deleted)" suffix; callers must handle inaccessible companion
files rather than treating the path as proof that its directory still exists.
